			"updated": 1779011437168,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "ELEVATED LDH",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 6,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "ELEVATED LDH",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"rawText": ""
		},
		{
			"id": "s5_b",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 28.32697599999983,
			"y": 1554.5159939999999,
			"width": 522.9197181230757,
			"height": 511.3727014576776,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 0,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b0H",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 567270636,
			"version": 565,
			"versionNonce": 1029317698,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1779011435869,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Context-first: ubiquitous enzyme — always correlate with clinical picture.\n\nSTEP 1: What is the scenario?\n\n1. ACLI / Ischemic Hepatitis:\n   - LDH rises 10-20x ULN in d1-3\n   - ALT:LDH <1.5 -> cardiogenic over viral\n   - Often precedes transaminase elevation\n\n2. Hemolysis (mechanical):\n   - LDH up + haptoglobin down\n   + indirect bili up + retics up\n   - LVAD, ECMO, valves, DIC\n\n3. Tissue ischemia / infarction:\n   - MI: LDH-1 > LDH-2 (flipped pattern)\n   - PE: LDH-3 predominance\n   - Mesenteric ischemia: LDH-5\n\nSTEP 2: LDH vs Serum Lactate — KEY DISTINCTION\n   LDH = enzyme (tissue injury marker)\n   Lactate = metabolite (hypoperfusion marker)\n   - Serial lactate trajectories (not single values) best predict shock outcomes\n   - Early clearance = reversible shock\n   - Persistent elevation = refractory shock OR impaired hepatic lactate clearance\n\nSTEP 3: Prognostic significance\n   GALACTIC-HF: Higher LDH independently predicts worse outcomes (Q4 vs Q1: HR 1.84 for\nHF event or CV death) Improves PREDICT-HF model accuracy\n\nLOW LDH: Rarely significant.\nLDH-A or LDH-B deficiency (rare genetic).\nNot actionable in the ICU setting.",
			"fontSize": 12.396913974731579,
			"fontFamily": 6,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Context-first: ubiquitous enzyme — always correlate with clinical picture.\n\nSTEP 1: What is the scenario?\n\n1. ACLI / Ischemic Hepatitis:\n   - LDH rises 10-20x ULN in d1-3\n   - ALT:LDH <1.5 -> cardiogenic over viral\n   - Often precedes transaminase elevation\n\n2. Hemolysis (mechanical):\n   - LDH up + haptoglobin down\n   + indirect bili up + retics up\n   - LVAD, ECMO, valves, DIC\n\n3. Tissue ischemia / infarction:\n   - MI: LDH-1 > LDH-2 (flipped pattern)\n   - PE: LDH-3 predominance\n   - Mesenteric ischemia: LDH-5\n\nSTEP 2: LDH vs Serum Lactate — KEY DISTINCTION\n   LDH = enzyme (tissue injury marker)\n   Lactate = metabolite (hypoperfusion marker)\n   - Serial lactate trajectories (not single values) best predict shock outcomes\n   - Early clearance = reversible shock\n   - Persistent elevation = refractory shock OR impaired hepatic lactate clearance\n\nSTEP 3: Prognostic significance\n   GALACTIC-HF: Higher LDH independently predicts worse outcomes (Q4 vs Q1: HR 1.84 for HF event or CV death) Improves PREDICT-HF model accuracy\n\nLOW LDH: Rarely significant.\nLDH-A or LDH-B deficiency (rare genetic).\nNot actionable in the ICU setting.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"rawText": "Context-first: ubiquitous enzyme — always correlate with clinical picture.\n\nSTEP 1: What is the scenario?\n\n1. ACLI / Ischemic Hepatitis:\n   - LDH rises 10-20x ULN in d1-3\n   - ALT:LDH <1.5 -> cardiogenic over viral\n   - Often precedes transaminase elevation\n\n2. Hemolysis (mechanical):\n   - LDH up + haptoglobin down\n   + indirect bili up + retics up\n   - LVAD, ECMO, valves, DIC\n\n3. Tissue ischemia / infarction:\n   - MI: LDH-1 > LDH-2 (flipped pattern)\n   - PE: LDH-3 predominance\n   - Mesenteric ischemia: LDH-5\n\nSTEP 2: LDH vs Serum Lactate — KEY DISTINCTION\n   LDH = enzyme (tissue injury marker)\n   Lactate = metabolite (hypoperfusion marker)\n   - Serial lactate trajectories (not single values) best predict shock outcomes\n   - Early clearance = reversible shock\n   - Persistent elevation = refractory shock OR impaired hepatic lactate clearance\n\nSTEP 3: Prognostic significance\n   GALACTIC-HF: Higher LDH independently predicts worse outcomes (Q4 vs Q1: HR 1.84 for HF event or CV death) Improves PREDICT-HF model accuracy\n\nLOW LDH: Rarely significant.\nLDH-A or LDH-B deficiency (rare genetic).\nNot actionable in the ICU setting.",
			"link": null
		},
