			"text": "LOW ALBUMIN — present in ~32% of HF patients",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 6,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "LOW ALBUMIN — present in ~32% of HF patients",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"rawText": ""
		},
		{
			"id": "s6_sub",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 648.3269759999998,
			"y": 1554.5159939999999,
			"width": 304.49859619140625,
			"height": 32.5,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#155e75",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 0,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b0L",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 647401964,
			"version": 221,
			"versionNonce": 700485214,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1779011437169,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Half-life ~20 days -> reflects CHRONIC dysfunction\n(vs INR which reflects ACUTE synthetic failure)",
			"fontSize": 13,
			"fontFamily": 6,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Half-life ~20 days -> reflects CHRONIC dysfunction\n(vs INR which reflects ACUTE synthetic failure)",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"rawText": ""
		},
		{
			"id": "s6_b",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 648.3269759999998,
			"y": 1592.5159939999999,
			"width": 538.1473020867151,
			"height": 390,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 0,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b0M",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 558158548,
			"version": 418,
			"versionNonce": 2107314846,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1779011437169,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "STEP 1: Contributing mechanisms (multifactorial)\n- Hepatic congestion -> impaired synthesis\n- Systemic inflammation -> cytokine-mediated suppression of synthesis (primary driver,\nmore important than malnutrition alone)\n- Malnutrition / cardiac cachexia\n- Hemodilution -> volume overload\n- Protein-losing enteropathy from elevated RAP\n- Nephrotic syndrome -> check urine protein\n\nSTEP 2: Workup\n- LFTs, INR (synthetic function panel)\n- Urine protein / albumin:creatinine ratio\n- CRP / ESR (inflammation burden)\n- Nutritional assessment (prealbumin)\n- Echo: IVC, RAP (dilution vs true impairment)\n\nSTEP 3: Management\n- Optimize decongestion (reduces congestion and hemodilution simultaneously)\n- Optimize oral nutrition (improved outcomes in malnourished HF patients)\n- IV albumin: controversial; routine use does NOT improve outcomes. Consider selectively\nfor refractory edema/ascites + profound hypoalbuminemia on max diuretics\n\nELEVATED ALBUMIN: Almost always dehydration\nor hemoconcentration. Reassess volume status.",
			"fontSize": 13,
			"fontFamily": 6,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "STEP 1: Contributing mechanisms (multifactorial)\n- Hepatic congestion -> impaired synthesis\n- Systemic inflammation -> cytokine-mediated suppression of synthesis (primary driver, more important than malnutrition alone)\n- Malnutrition / cardiac cachexia\n- Hemodilution -> volume overload\n- Protein-losing enteropathy from elevated RAP\n- Nephrotic syndrome -> check urine protein\n\nSTEP 2: Workup\n- LFTs, INR (synthetic function panel)\n- Urine protein / albumin:creatinine ratio\n- CRP / ESR (inflammation burden)\n- Nutritional assessment (prealbumin)\n- Echo: IVC, RAP (dilution vs true impairment)\n\nSTEP 3: Management\n- Optimize decongestion (reduces congestion and hemodilution simultaneously)\n- Optimize oral nutrition (improved outcomes in malnourished HF patients)\n- IV albumin: controversial; routine use does NOT improve outcomes. Consider selectively for refractory edema/ascites + profound hypoalbuminemia on max diuretics\n\nELEVATED ALBUMIN: Almost always dehydration\nor hemoconcentration. Reassess volume status.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"rawText": "STEP 1: Contributing mechanisms (multifactorial)\n- Hepatic congestion -> impaired synthesis\n- Systemic inflammation -> cytokine-mediated suppression of synthesis (primary driver, more important than malnutrition alone)\n- Malnutrition / cardiac cachexia\n- Hemodilution -> volume overload\n- Protein-losing enteropathy from elevated RAP\n- Nephrotic syndrome -> check urine protein\n\nSTEP 2: Workup\n- LFTs, INR (synthetic function panel)\n- Urine protein / albumin:creatinine ratio\n- CRP / ESR (inflammation burden)\n- Nutritional assessment (prealbumin)\n- Echo: IVC, RAP (dilution vs true impairment)\n\nSTEP 3: Management\n- Optimize decongestion (reduces congestion and hemodilution simultaneously)\n- Optimize oral nutrition (improved outcomes in malnourished HF patients)\n- IV albumin: controversial; routine use does NOT improve outcomes. Consider selectively for refractory edema/ascites + profound hypoalbuminemia on max diuretics\n\nELEVATED ALBUMIN: Almost always dehydration\nor hemoconcentration. Reassess volume status.",
			"link": null
		},
